%[text] %[text:anchor:T_F10A6C38] # Fluorescent Micrograph Segmentation
%[text] Fluorescent Color Images (with multiple channels) often require a different approach for segmentation than RGB images. This is why it is important to know what kind of image you are starting with
%[text] - **RGB images**: Treat all three channels as one unit. You need to collect the color information from all channels.
%[text] - **Fluorescent Images**: treat each channel separately. Index out the channel and treat as a grayscale image. Combine the masks after segmentation   \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Multichannel Images
%[text] This image is a 3 Channel micrograph of bovine pulmonary artery endothelial cells with the following staining: 
%[text] -  **Blue**: nucleus stained with DAPI
%[text] -  **Green**: Tubulin stained with Bodipy FL goat anti-mouse IgG
%[text] -  **Red**: F-Actin stained with Texas Red X-Phalloidin \
%[text] For this image, our approach will be to segment each channel separately. 
%%
%[text] ### Load Image
clearvars
mmSetUnitDataFolder(2) %[output:85caa880]
p.img = imread("FluorescentCells.png");

figure %[output:51a275b2]
imshow(p.img) %[output:51a275b2]
title('Flourescent Cells') %[output:51a275b2]
%%
%[text] ### Review Channels and histograms
%[text] Its always a good idea to inspect each channel and review the histograms
figure %[output:7fd9574c]
mmShowHist(p.img) %[output:7fd9574c]
%[text] - Looks like pretty good contrast across all channels
%[text] - Let's get into it \
%%
%[text] ### Segment the blue channel
%[text] The blue channel looks the most straightforward
b.ch = p.img(:,:,3); % index out the blue channel
b.mask = imbinarize(b.ch);
figure %[output:8bb0779e]
imshowpair(imadjust(b.ch),b.mask) %[output:8bb0779e]
%[text] - yep, pretty easy
%[text] - Notice the mask covers all the DAPI signal in the channel \
%%
%[text] A simple open operation cleans up the small noise
b.mask = bwareaopen(b.mask,50); % remove noise smaller than 50 pxls
imshowpair(b.ch,b.mask) %[output:3c313350]
%%
%[text] ### Segment the Red Channel
%[text] Ok, how about the red channel?
r.ch = p.img(:,:,1);
r.mask = imbinarize(r.ch);
imshowpair(r.ch, r.mask) %[output:948ff4ee]
title('Red Channel Segmentation')
%[text] - Again, pretty easy \
%%
%[text] ### Segment Green Channel
%[text] We use the same basic approach for the green channel
g.ch = p.img(:,:,2);
g.mask = imbinarize(g.ch);
imshowpair(g.ch, g.mask) %[output:49d44e77]
%[text] - for this segmentation, we are missing some of the signal \
%%
%[text] #### Review the channel
figure %[output:115e6bf4]
imshow(g.ch) %[output:115e6bf4]
%[text] - notice the uneven staining
%[text] - Some of the stain is really bright, and some less so \
%%
%[text] Hmm, maybe we need to increase the contrast. Let's try `imadjust`. And filter the image with a median fillter
g.adj = imadjust(g.ch); % enhance contrast
g.greenf = medfilt2(g.adj); % apply median filter
g.adj_mask = imbinarize(g.greenf); % create mask
imshowpair(g.ch, g.adj_mask) %[output:085a8495]
%[text] - Still missing some signal \
%%
%[text] There are some really bright spots in the signal. Can `adapthist` succeed where `imadjust` failed? Remember adapthist works locally with a kernel, which can help improve contrast locally
g.adapt = adapthisteq(g.ch); % use instead of imadjust

g.greenf = medfilt2(g.adapt); 
g.gmask = imbinarize(g.greenf);
imshowpair(g.ch, g.gmask) %[output:173648e8]
%[text] - Pretty good. `adapthist` for the win.  \
%%
%[text] #### Review steps
figure %[output:2d4f47e5]
mmShowStruct(g) %[output:2d4f47e5]
%%
%[text] ### Combine masks and display
rgbMask = single(cat(3,r.mask,g.mask,b.mask));
figure;
mmTightTiledLayout %[output:58ef9ca6]
nexttile %[output:58ef9ca6]
imshow(p.img) %[output:58ef9ca6]

nexttile %[output:58ef9ca6]
imshow(rgbMask) %[output:58ef9ca6] %[output:4ce50c01] %[output:70ac31ca]
%%
%[text] What's the percent overlap of the green and red masks?

%%
%[text] ### SEGMENT Olfactory bulb 
%[text] In this example, we will load an image generated for SCIENCE. This image was captured from the brain of a mouse using techniques similar, but not exactly as shown here. 
%[text] The anatomy in question is the Main Olfactory bulb, which receives input the olfactory epithelium found at the back of the nose. The MOB is covered in spheres of neuropil (tangles of synaptic-dendritic connections) called glomeruli. The activation of this glomeruli can inform us on how olfactory information is encoded in the brain. In this experiment, activity was monitored by recording calcium activation using the fluorescent  dye known as Fura. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_52689580] ![](text:image:89fb)![](text:image:52e5)![](text:image:7b51)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_52229AE4] ## Segment Image
%[text] ### Read image
%[text] Select the Fura1.tiff  data folder
clearvars
clc
close all

mmSetUnitDataFolder(2) %[output:6b0ec8a5]
p.img = imread("Fura1.tiff"); % read file
meta = imfinfo("Fura1.tiff") % read metadata %[output:02f47536]
%[text] - inspect meta-data before proceeding
%[text] - Bit depth? \
%%
%[text] What's the maximum intensity in the image?
size(p.img) %[output:72b9d77a]
max(p.img(:)) %[output:69acad57]
log2(5812) %[output:5b365e3b]
%[text] -  Is this a 16-bit image?
%[text] - Will we be able to see anything? \
%%
%[text] ### Display Image
%[text] with no correction
figure %[output:90262411]
imshow(p.img) %[output:90262411]
%[text] - We can't see anything \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E7D0E376] ### Display Image and  histogram
%[text] - use mmShowHist  \
mmShowHist(p.img) %[output:709722db]
%[text] - Reviewing the histogram, can you see why the image basically displays as black?
%[text] - Note: `mmShowHist` automatically scales the image (`imshow(img,[])`, which is why we can see the image here.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_00C7241F] ### Enhance Contrast to review image
%[text] - We can adjust the image using `imadjust`
%[text] - `mmShowHist` displays the image and histogram \
p.adjust = imadjust(p.img);
figure; %[output:9a100f53]
mmShowHist(p.adjust) %[output:9a100f53]
%[text] - Now we can see the image, but...
%[text] - What is good or bad about using **imadjust** for image processing?
%[text] - Oversaturated images may interfere with picking the right threshold \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8771F90] ### Normalize image
%[text] - By changing the intensity scale, normalizing can help make the image visible
%[text] - Here we use **mat2gray** to rescale the image from 0 to 1, where the max intensity is 1.  \
p.imgn = mat2gray(p.img);
figure %[output:8339510e]
mmShowHist(p.imgn) %[output:8339510e]
%[text] - Now we can see the image — can you see why?
%[text] - Are there more or less  saturated pixels? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AB453CDC] ## Segmentation
%[text] Ok, we're ready to segment. Let's review the enhance image to plan our strategy. We'll use this enhanced image  p.imga when we want to review our results
figure
mmTightTiledLayout %[output:03344c02]
nexttile
imshow(p.adjust)
title("imadjust")
%[text] - notice that this image has uneven illumination: the background is unevenly lit, where some of the background is almost as bright as some of the dots that we are trying to capture
%[text] - Also note we haven't overwritten ***imgn,*** we just enhanced it for display purposes \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D7132C91] ### binarize
%[text] Since the image is uneven illumination, we should use the adaptive setting and sensitivity setting
sens = 0.2; % default = 0.2 %[output:3cef0d5b]
p.mask = imbinarize(p.imgn,'adaptive','Sensitivity',0.2);
p.mask = bwareaopen(p.mask,50);
nexttile %[output:77665d6f]
imshowpair(p.adjust,p.mask) %[output:77665d6f]
title(sprintf('Sensitivity = %.2f',sens)) %[output:77665d6f]
%[text] - try adjusting the sensitivity \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F7513678] ## Preprocessing the image
%[text] Notice that we didn't capture all of the glomeruli. We can add some preprocessing steps to improve our results.
%[text] - use a median filter on the normalized image
%[text] - apply a gaussian filter on the filtered image with a sigma of 3 \
%[text] For example, we can use a median filter to help clean up some of the noise. And a gaussian blur to enhance the contrast between the dots and the background
p.adapt = adapthisteq(p.imgn);
p.medf = medfilt2(p.adapt);
p.gaussf = imgaussfilt(p.medf,3);

figure; % visualize and review filtering %[output:6fd95719]
tiledlayout("horizontal", TileSpacing="none") %[output:6fd95719]
for n=["adjust" "adapt" "medf" "gaussf"] %[output:group:9f09c419]
    nexttile %[output:6fd95719]
    imshow(p.(n))
    title(n)
end %[output:group:9f09c419]
%[text] - Median Filter cleans up the noise
%[text] -  the gaussian filtering helps smooth out some of the graininess in the original image \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_29BAA495] ### Binarize the filtered image
%[text] - use adaptive with a sensitivity of 0.1
%[text] - clean up noise smaller than 75 \
p.mask = imbinarize(p.gaussf,"adaptive","Sensitivity",0.1);
p.mask = bwareaopen(p.mask,75);
p.mask = imclearborder(p.mask);

figure;
mmTightTiledLayout %[output:24950fc2]
nexttile %[output:24950fc2]
imshow(p.adjust) %[output:24950fc2]
title("imadjust") %[output:24950fc2]

nexttile %[output:24950fc2]
imshowpair(p.adjust,p.mask) %[output:24950fc2]
title("mask adjust overlay") %[output:24950fc2]
%[text] - works  pretty nice \
%%
p.maskClean = bwareaopen(p.mask,200);
p.
nexttile %[output:758edb66]
imshow(p.maskClean) %[output:758edb66]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9F25AF05] ### Separate touching glomeruli
%[text] - watershed: use a setting of 2, following by a setting of 1 \
p.maskWater = mmGetWatershed(p.maskClean,ShowSteps=true); % remember to assign to mask after you have the right settings
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4B787A0C] ### Estimate Microns per pixel
imageViewer(p.maskWater)
%%
umperpx = 28/72
%[text] - remember, you should rarely have to do this \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_338EC9F7] ## REGION PROPERTIES 
rp = regionprops('table',p.maskWater,p.imgn,{'Area', 'Centroid', 'EquivDiameter','BoundingBox', 'Perimeter','Circularity','MeanIntensity'})
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2F360814] ### convert to um
%[text] How would you convert area to µm
rp.Area_um = rp.Area * umperpx^2
%%
%[text] How about equivalent diameter?
rp.ED_um = rp.EquivDiameter * umperpx;
%[text] - When its a line, you just multiply by umperpx \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9D6B370A] ### Group summary
%[text] Calculate the mean area in µm^2 and mean diameter in micros
ts = groupsummary(rp,[],["mean","std"],["Area_um","ED_um"])
%%
%[text] Display boxcharts of area and equivalent diameters with the units converted to µms
figure
nexttile
boxchart(rp.Area_um)
ylabel("Area (µm^2)")

nexttile
boxchart(rp.ED_um)
ylabel("Diameter (µm)")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9D0E4E40] ### Label all CCs with their intensities
figure;
imshowpair(p.imgn, p.maskWater)

for n=1:height(rp)
    xy = rp.Centroid(n,:);
    text(xy(1), xy(2), sprintf('%1.2f',rp.MeanIntensity(n)),'Color','y','FontWeight', 'bold') % adds text
end
%%
%[text] Are the Brighter dots larger?
figure
scatter(rp.Area,rp.MeanIntensity)
%%
corr(rp.Area,rp.MeanIntensity)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_566CD650] Are Brighter dots rounder?
figure;
scatter(rp.Circularity,rp.MeanIntensity)
corr(rp.Circularity,rp.MeanIntensity)

